1
00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:08.000
By now, you already get that these agents are very powerful, and they're good at making decisions on the fly and reacting to anything that you throw at them.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:17.000
But sometimes you want more control. Sometimes you want to make sure that you execute actual code either prior or after these agents are executed.

3
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
And this is where execution hooks come in.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:33.000
It allows you to write code that is going to run right before your agents start doing work, or right after, and use that information either to input data into the agents or to take data out of these agents and send them somewhere else.

5
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.000
This is a very interesting technique and can play a huge role depending on your use case.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.000
So let me show you how it's done.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
So by now, you're getting very proficient with crews.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:55.000
And you probably already identified that whenever you have a crew, you might want to have something to happen right before it starts doing its work or right after it's done with its work.

9
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
And that can be so many different things.

10
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000
A lot of times it is loading data from somewhere or loading data off to somewhere.

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
And you might want to use that data to change some of the information.

12
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:17.000
So in most cases, you might want to have this something happen that modifies the inputs that goes into the agent or modifies the outputs that are going out of your crew.

13
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
So you might want to change this information in certain ways or combine it with something else before you start your workflow.

14
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
And by the end of the workflow, you might want to push that final result such as into Salesforce or another system or even your email.

15
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.000
And that's where Hooks comes in.

16
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Because either before or after Hooks will allow you to write code that you want to be executed right in there.

17
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:48.000
It's almost like an easy step before you go all the way into a flow where you already start to bring in some actual coding into your agents.

18
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.000
So a before Hook, for example, might be fetching input data or cleaning input data, checking for PII or personal information and make sure that it's scraped those out.

19
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:11.000
While after Hooks will be validating some of these outputs, will be moderating content, might be also doing things like removing PII or even just logging outputs somewhere so that you can inspect them later.

20
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
A good example of a pre-Hook function would be something like this.

21
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Let's say that part of the inputs is a phone number and you want to make sure that you're formatting it right.

22
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:32.000
So what you do in here is you extract this information and use regular Python to basically break it down in a very specific format and then reintroduce that into the inputs.

23
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So that's something that you can do to make sure that the data going into the crew and into your agents is properly formatted.

24
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.000
And this is an example of what would be a good post-Hook function where now you're getting the outputs.

25
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.000
And let's say that the output now also includes a phone number, but you want to hide that away.

26
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
So you might basically implement something that hides the few numbers and only show the final four numbers.

27
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:04.000
So obfuscating part of that output and then you're returning that output forward.

28
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Now, if you want to add those pre-Hooks and these post-Hooks into your tasks, all you got to do in the code is add these two new attributes, a pre-Hook and a post-Hook.

29
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:27.000
And you can see that both of them take arrays as values, meaning that you can add many different hooks either to be run before or after the execution, allowing you to have a lot of control into that.

30
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:39.000
So in the end of the day, you can see how hooks either before or after hooks can actually play a huge role on helping you build reliable systems.

31
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:47.000
If there's one thing that you are going to hear me referencing over and over is that in order for the systems to get into production, they need to be reliable.

32
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.000
You can definitely build them extremely easily with Crew, both with no code and pro code.

33
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:02.000
But when you get to ship them, you want to make sure that you are giving outputs that are consistent and they're filtering out any information that is going in or out.

34
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:10.000
So in the end of the day, a lot of the focus on hooks is around this idea of building reliable systems.

35
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:17.000
Now, the utmost control that you can gather with CrewAI is when you go to CrewAI Flows.

36
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
And we're going to talk a lot about that in Module 3.

37
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:32.000
And the idea here is that flows will allow you to merge actual code with either one single LLM calls, one single agent or an entire crew together.

38
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
That is the ultimate backbone that can bring everything together.

39
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.000
And we're going to dive deep into Flows in the few lessons ahead.

40
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:46.000
And I'm already start to putting some of this context and some of these concepts together for you.

41
